      "expectation": "DETECT",
      "id": "g3-fault-recompute-circular-dependency",
      "reason": "a circular dependency cannot be planted through the production path. Measured on FreeCAD 1.1.0 by running the op through score_recompute_infc.run_case: Cut already consumes Base, so `Base.setExpression('Height', 'Cut.Shape.BoundBox.ZLength')` closes the loop, and FreeCAD raises RuntimeError: cyclic reference to <doc>#Cut at setExpression time, inside the op. The bracket maps that to SPEC_FAILED and rolls back, leaving Base, Tool and Cut all ['Up-to-date'] 'Valid' and verify_recompute reporting verdict=OK with failing_objects=[]. The cycle is refused before it exists, so there is no document state for the validator to read. This is FreeCAD's own guard working, not a validator miss"
    },
    {
      "expectation": "DETECT",
      "id": "g3-fault-recompute-parameter-removed-under-binding",
      "reason": "removing a driving parameter from its holder plants no fault. Measured on FreeCAD 1.1.0 by running the op through score_recompute_infc.run_case: `parameters.declare` binds Box.Height by expression to Parameters.plate_thickness, then `holder.removeProperty('plate_thickness')` inside the production bracket commits OK and leaves the document at Box=['Up-to-date'] 'Valid', Parameters=['Up-to-date'] 'Valid' with verify_recompute reporting verdict=OK and failing_objects=[]. FreeCAD does not re-evaluate a bound expression when the property it names is dropped, so no object enters error state. Scoring this as a miss would blame verify_recompute for a defect that was never created; the real gap is that removeProperty leaves a stale binding silently, which is a finding about parameters.py and not about this validator"
    }
  ],
